{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"heptapod","tool":"Inspiresearch","arguments":{"query":"scalar leptoquark S1","sort":"mostcited","size":5},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"schema\": \"inspire-hep-1.0\",\n  \"total\": 20,\n  \"returned\": 5,\n  \"papers\": [\n    {\n      \"recid\": \"1856811\",\n      \"title\": \"New physics explanations of a$_{\\u03bc}$ in light of the FNAL muon g \\u2212 2 measurement\",\n      \"authors\": [\n        \"Athron, Peter\",\n        \"Bal\\u00e1zs, Csaba\",\n        \"Jacob, Douglas H.J.\",\n        \"Kotlarski, Wojciech\",\n        \"St\\u00f6ckinger, Dominik\",\n        \"St\\u00f6ckinger-Kim, Hyejung\"\n      ],\n      \"abstract\": \"The Fermilab Muon $g-2$ experiment recently reported its first measurement of the anomalous magnetic moment $a_\\\\mu^{\\\\textrm{FNAL}}$, which is in full agreement with the previous BNL measurement and pushes the world average deviation $\\\\Delta a_\\\\mu^{2021}$ from the Standard Model to a significance of $4.2\\\\sigma$. Here we provide an extensive survey of its impact on beyond the Standard Model physics. We use state-of-the-art calculations and a sophisticated set of tools to make predictions for $a_\\\\mu$, dark matter and LHC searches in a wide range of simple models with up to three new fields, that represent some of the few ways that large $\\\\Delta a_\\\\mu$ can be explained. In addition for the particularly well motivated Minimal Supersymmetric Standard Model, we exhaustively cover the scenarios where large $\\\\Delta a_\\\\mu$ can be explained while simultaneously satisfying all relevant data from other experiments. Generally, the $\\\\Delta a_\\\\mu$ result can only be explained by rather small masses and/or large couplings and enhanced chirality flips, which can lead to conflicts with limits from LHC and dark matter experiments. Our results show that the new measurement excludes a large number of models and provides crucial constraints on others. Two-Higgs doublet and leptoquark models provide viable explanations of $a_\\\\mu$ only in specific versions and in specific parameter ranges. Among all models with up to three fields, only models with chirality enhancements can accommodate $a_\\\\mu$ and dark matter simultaneously. The MSSM can simultaneously explain $a_\\\\mu$ and dark matter for Bino-like LSP in several coannihilation regions. Allowing under abundance of the dark matter relic density, the Higgsino- and particularly Wino-like LSP scenarios become promising explanations of the $a_\\\\mu$ result.\",\n      \"arxiv_id\": \"2104.03691\",\n      \"arxiv_category\": \"hep-ph\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1007/JHEP09(2021)080\",\n      \"publication_info\": \"JHEP 09 (2021) 080\",\n      \"journal\": \"JHEP\",\n      \"volume\": \"09\",\n      \"year\": 2021,\n      \"pages\": \"080\",\n      \"citation_count\": 304,\n      \"citation_count_without_self\": 290,\n      \"date\": \"2021-04-08\",\n      \"inspire_url\": \"https://inspirehep.net/literature/1856811\",\n      \"arxiv_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.03691\",\n      \"keywords\": [\n        \"new physics\",\n        \"leptoquark\",\n        \"Higgs particle: doublet: 2\",\n        \"supersymmetry\",\n        \"minimal supersymmetric standard model\",\n        \"Brookhaven Lab\",\n        \"muon: magnetic moment\",\n        \"magnetic moment: anomaly\",\n        \"bino: dark matter\",\n        \"bino: LSP\",\n        \"LSP: dark matter\",\n        \"dark matter: relic density\",\n        \"Beyond Standard Model\",\n        \"Supersymmetric Standard Model\"\n      ],\n      \"document_type\": \"article\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"recid\": \"1429667\",\n      \"title\": \"LHC constraints and prospects for $S_1$ scalar leptoquark explaining the $\\\\bar B \\\\to D^{(*)} \\\\tau \\\\bar\\\\nu$ anomaly\",\n      \"authors\": [\n        \"Dumont, B\\u00e9ranger\",\n        \"Nishiwaki, Kenji\",\n        \"Watanabe, Ryoutaro\"\n      ],\n      \"abstract\": \"Recently, deviations in flavor observables of B\\u00af\\u2192D(*)\\u03c4\\u03bd\\u00af have been shown between the predictions in the Standard Model and the experimental results reported by BABAR, Belle, and LHCb collaborations. One of the solutions to this anomaly is obtained in a class of leptoquark model with a scalar leptoquark boson S1, which is a SU(3)c triplet and SU(2)L singlet particle with -1/3 hypercharge interacting with a quark-lepton pair. With well-adjusted couplings, this model can explain the anomaly and be compatible with all flavor constraints. In such a case, the S1 boson can be pair-produced at CERN\\u2019s Large Hadron Collider (LHC) and subsequently decay as S1*\\u2192t\\u03c4, b\\u03bd\\u03c4, and c\\u03c4. This paper explores the current 8 and 13\\u00a0TeV constraints, as well as the detailed prospects at 14\\u00a0TeV, of this flavor-motivated S1 model. From the current available 8 and 13\\u00a0TeV LHC searches, we obtain constraints on the S1 boson mass for MS1<400\\u2013640\\u2009\\u2009GeV depending on values of the leptoquark couplings to fermions. Then we study future prospects for this scenario at the 14\\u00a0TeV LHC using detailed cut analyses and evaluate exclusion and discovery potentials for the flavor-motivated S1 leptoquark model from searches for the (b\\u03bd)(b\\u00af\\u03bd\\u00af) and (c\\u03c4)(c\\u00af\\u03c4\\u00af) final states. In the latter case, we consider several scenarios for the identification of charm jets. As a result, we find that the S1 leptoquark origin of the B\\u00af\\u2192D(*)\\u03c4\\u03bd\\u00af anomaly can be probed with MS1\\u2272600/800\\u2009\\u2009GeV at the 14\\u00a0TeV LHC with L=300/3000\\u2009\\u2009fb-1 of accumulated data. One can also see that the 14\\u00a0TeV LHC run II with L=300\\u2009\\u2009fb-1 can exclude the S1 leptoquark boson up to MS1\\u223c0.8\\u2009\\u2009TeV at 95% confidence level, whereas a future 14\\u00a0TeV LHC with L=3000\\u2009\\u2009fb-1 data has a potential to discover the S1 leptoquark boson with its mass up to MS1\\u223c1.1\\u2009\\u2009TeV with over 5\\u03c3 significance, from the (b\\u03bd)(b\\u00af\\u03bd\\u00af) and/or (c\\u03c4)(c\\u00af\\u03c4\\u00af) searches.\",\n      \"arxiv_id\": \"1603.05248\",\n      \"arxiv_category\": \"hep-ph\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1103/PhysRevD.94.034001\",\n      \"publication_info\": \"Phys.Rev.D 94 (2016)\",\n      \"journal\": \"Phys.Rev.D\",\n      \"volume\": \"94\",\n      \"year\": 2016,\n      \"citation_count\": 113,\n      \"citation_count_without_self\": 104,\n      \"date\": \"2016-03-16\",\n      \"inspire_url\": \"https://inspirehep.net/literature/1429667\",\n      \"arxiv_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.05248\",\n      \"keywords\": [\n        \"B: leptonic decay\",\n        \"B: branching ratio\",\n        \"branching ratio: ratio\",\n        \"leptoquark: scalar\",\n        \"leptoquark: coupling\",\n        \"jet: charm\",\n        \"BELLE\",\n        \"LHC-B\",\n        \"BaBar\",\n        \"CERN Lab\",\n        \"CERN LHC Coll\",\n        \"numerical calculations\",\n        \"new physics\"\n      ],\n      \"document_type\": \"article\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"recid\": \"1783001\",\n      \"title\": \"Getting chirality right: Single scalar leptoquark solutions to the $(g-2)_{e,\\\\mu}$ puzzle\",\n      \"authors\": [\n        \"Bigaran, Innes\",\n        \"Volkas, Raymond R.\"\n      ],\n      \"abstract\": \"We identify the two scalar leptoquarks capable of generating sign-dependent contributions to leptonic magnetic moments, R2\\u223c(3,2,7/6) and S1\\u223c(3,1,-1/3), as favored by current measurements. We consider the case in which the electron and muon sectors are decoupled, and real-valued Yukawa couplings are specified using an up-type quark mass-diagonal basis. Contributions to \\u0394ae arise from charm-containing loops and \\u0394a\\u03bc from top-containing loops\\u2014hence avoiding dangerous LFV constraints, particularly from \\u03bc\\u2192e\\u03b3. The strongest constraints on these models arise from contributions to the Z leptonic decay widths, high-pT leptonic tails at the LHC, and from (semi)leptonic kaon decays. To be a comprehensive solution to the (g-2)e/\\u03bc puzzle we find that the mass of either leptoquark must be \\u227265\\u2009\\u2009TeV. This analysis can be embedded within broader flavor anomaly studies, including those of hierarchical leptoquark coupling structures. It can also be straightforwardly adapted to accommodate future measurements of leptonic magnetic moments, such as those expected from the Muon g-2 collaboration in the near future.\",\n      \"arxiv_id\": \"2002.12544\",\n      \"arxiv_category\": \"hep-ph\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1103/PhysRevD.102.075037\",\n      \"publication_info\": \"Phys.Rev.D 102 (2020)\",\n      \"journal\": \"Phys.Rev.D\",\n      \"volume\": \"102\",\n      \"year\": 2020,\n      \"citation_count\": 107,\n      \"citation_count_without_self\": 102,\n      \"date\": \"2020-03-09\",\n      \"inspire_url\": \"https://inspirehep.net/literature/1783001\",\n      \"arxiv_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.12544\",\n      \"keywords\": [\n        \"Beyond the standard model\",\n        \"new physics\",\n        \"leptoquark: scalar\",\n        \"leptoquark: coupling\",\n        \"lepton: magnetic moment\",\n        \"muon: magnetic moment\",\n        \"Z0: leptonic decay\",\n        \"hierarchy\",\n        \"texture\",\n        \"anomaly\",\n        \"flavor\",\n        \"spin: dependence\",\n        \"loop integral\",\n        \"top: coupling\"\n      ],\n      \"document_type\": \"article\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"recid\": \"1802353\",\n      \"title\": \"$\\\\mu \\\\to e \\\\gamma$  selecting scalar leptoquark solutions for the $(g-2)_{e,\\\\mu}$ puzzles\",\n      \"authors\": [\n        \"Dor\\u0161ner, Ilja\",\n        \"Fajfer, Svjetlana\",\n        \"Saad, Shaikh\"\n      ],\n      \"abstract\": \"We investigate all potentially viable scenarios that can produce the chiral enhancement required to simultaneously explain the (g-2)e and (g-2)\\u03bc data with either a single scalar leptoquark or a pair of scalar leptoquarks. We provide a classification of these scenarios in terms of their ability to satisfy the existing limits on the branching ratio for the \\u03bc\\u2192e\\u03b3 process. The simultaneous explanation of the (g-2)e,\\u03bc discrepancies, coupled with the current experimental data, implies that the (g-2)e loops are exclusively due to the charm-quark propagation, whereas the (g-2)\\u03bc loops are due to the top-quark propagation. The scenarios where the (g-2)e loops are due to the top (bottom) quark propagation are, at best, approximately 9 (3)\\u00a0orders of magnitude away from the experimental limit on the \\u03bc\\u2192e\\u03b3 branching ratio. All in all, there are only three particular scenarios that can pass the \\u03bc\\u2192e\\u03b3 test and simultaneously create a large enough impact on the (g-2)e,\\u03bc discrepancies when the new physics is based on the Standard Model fermion content. These are the S1, R2, and S1&S3 scenarios, where the first two are already known to be phenomenologically viable candidates with respect to all other flavor and collider data constraints. We show that the third scenario\\u2014where the right-chiral couplings to charged leptons are due to S1, the left-chiral couplings to charged leptons are due to S3, and the two leptoquarks mix through the Standard Model Higgs field\\u2014cannot address the (g-2)e and (g-2)\\u03bc discrepancies at the 1\\u03c3 level due to an interplay between KL0\\u2192e\\u00b1\\u03bc\\u2213, Z\\u2192e+e-, and Z\\u2192\\u03bc+\\u03bc- data despite the ability of that scenario to avoid the \\u03bc\\u2192e\\u03b3 limit.\",\n      \"arxiv_id\": \"2006.11624\",\n      \"arxiv_category\": \"hep-ph\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1103/PhysRevD.102.075007\",\n      \"publication_info\": \"Phys.Rev.D 102 (2020)\",\n      \"journal\": \"Phys.Rev.D\",\n      \"volume\": \"102\",\n      \"year\": 2020,\n      \"citation_count\": 87,\n      \"citation_count_without_self\": 77,\n      \"date\": \"2020-06-20\",\n      \"inspire_url\": \"https://inspirehep.net/literature/1802353\",\n      \"arxiv_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.11624\",\n      \"keywords\": [\n        \"Beyond the standard model\",\n        \"lepton: flavor: violation\",\n        \"muon: decay\",\n        \"muon: magnetic moment\",\n        \"magnetic moment: anomaly\",\n        \"leptoquark: scalar\",\n        \"numerical calculations\",\n        \"new physics\",\n        \"muon- --> electron photon\"\n      ],\n      \"document_type\": \"article\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"recid\": \"1758211\",\n      \"title\": \"Muon $g-2$ and scalar leptoquark mixing\",\n      \"authors\": [\n        \"Dor\\u0161ner, Ilja\",\n        \"Fajfer, Svjetlana\",\n        \"Sumensari, Olcyr\"\n      ],\n      \"abstract\": \"The observed muon anomalous magnetic moment deviates from the Standard Model predictions. There are two scalar leptoquarks with simultaneous couplings to the quark-muon pairs of both chiralities that can singly explain this discrepancy. We discuss an alternative mechanism that calls for the mixing of two scalar leptoquarks of the same electric charge through the interaction with the Higgs field, where the two leptoquarks separately couple to the quark-muon pairs of opposite chirality structures. Three scenarios that satisfy this requirement are S$_{1}$ & S$_{3}$, $ {\\\\tilde{S}}_1 $ & S$_{3}$, and $ {\\\\tilde{R}}_2 $ & R$_{2}$, where the first scenario is realised with the up-type quarks running in the loops while the other two scenarios proceed through the down-type quark loops. We introduce only two non-zero Yukawa couplings to the relevant quarks and a muon, at the time, to study ability of these three scenarios to explain (g \\u2212 2)$_{\\u03bc}$ and be in accord with available experimental constraints. We find that the S$_{1}$ & S$_{3}$ scenario with the top-quark loops is consistent with all existing measurements. The $ {\\\\tilde{S}}_1 $ & S$_{3}$ and $ {\\\\tilde{R}}_2 $ & R$_{2}$ scenarios can accommodate the observed discrepancy through the bottom-quark loops but exhibit significant tension with the existing data on the high-p$_{T}$ dilepton-tails at LHC for the required values of Yukawa couplings and leptoquark masses.\",\n      \"arxiv_id\": \"1910.03877\",\n      \"arxiv_category\": \"hep-ph\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1007/JHEP06(2020)089\",\n      \"publication_info\": \"JHEP 06 (2020) 089\",\n      \"journal\": \"JHEP\",\n      \"volume\": \"06\",\n      \"year\": 2020,\n      \"pages\": \"089\",\n      \"citation_count\": 81,\n      \"citation_count_without_self\": 74,\n      \"date\": \"2019-10-09\",\n      \"inspire_url\": \"https://inspirehep.net/literature/1758211\",\n      \"arxiv_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.03877\",\n      \"keywords\": [\n        \"Beyond Standard Model\",\n        \"Heavy Quark Physics\",\n        \"leptoquark: scalar\",\n        \"coupling: Yukawa\",\n        \"muon: magnetic moment\",\n        \"leptoquark: mass\",\n        \"correction: oblique\",\n        \"charge: electric\",\n        \"mass: scale\",\n        \"mixing\",\n        \"quark\",\n        \"structure\",\n        \"charm\"\n      ],\n      \"document_type\": \"article\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"query_info\": {\n    \"original\": \"scalar leptoquark S1\",\n    \"spires\": \"scalar leptoquark S1\",\n    \"sort\": \"mostcited\",\n    \"size\": 5\n  }\n}"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
