{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"heptapod","tool":"Inspiresearch","arguments":{"query":"leptoquark standard model extension","sort":"mostcited","size":8},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"schema\": \"inspire-hep-1.0\",\n  \"total\": 107,\n  \"returned\": 8,\n  \"papers\": [\n    {\n      \"recid\": \"1817513\",\n      \"title\": \"Baryogenesis from the weak scale to the grand unification scale\",\n      \"authors\": [\n        \"Bodeker, Dietrich\",\n        \"Buchmuller, Wilfried\"\n      ],\n      \"abstract\": \"The current status of baryogenesis is reviewed, with an emphasis on electroweak baryogenesis and leptogenesis. The first detailed studies were carried out for <math display=\\\"inline\\\"><mrow><mi>SU</mi><mo stretchy=\\\"false\\\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\\\"false\\\">)</mo></mrow></math> grand unified theory (GUT) models where <math display=\\\"inline\\\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow></math>-violating decays of leptoquarks generate a baryon asymmetry. These GUT models were excluded by the discovery of unsuppressed, (<math display=\\\"inline\\\"><mi>B</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi></math>)-violating sphaleron processes at high temperatures. Yet a new possibility emerged: electroweak baryogenesis. Here sphaleron processes generate a baryon asymmetry during a strongly first-order phase transition. This mechanism has been studied in detail in many extensions of the standard model. However, constraints from the LHC and from low-energy precision experiments exclude most of the known models, leaving composite Higgs models of electroweak symmetry breaking as an interesting possibility. Sphaleron processes are also the basis of leptogenesis, where <math display=\\\"inline\\\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow></math>-violating decays of heavy right-handed neutrinos generate a lepton asymmetry that is partially converted to a baryon asymmetry. This mechanism is closely related to that of GUT baryogenesis, and simple estimates based on GUT models can explain the order of magnitude of the observed baryon-to-photon ratio. In the one-flavor approximation an upper bound on the light-neutrino masses has been derived that is consistent with the cosmological upper bound on the sum of neutrino masses. For quasidegenerate right-handed neutrinos the leptogenesis temperature can be lowered from the GUT scale down to the weak scale, and <math display=\\\"inline\\\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow></math>-violating oscillations of GeV sterile neutinos can also lead to successful leptogenesis. Significant progress has been made in developing a full field-theoretical description of thermal leptogenesis, which demonstrated that interactions with gauge bosons of the thermal plasma play a crucial role. Finally, recent ideas on how the seesaw mechanism and <math display=\\\"inline\\\"><mi>B</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>L</mi></math> breaking at the GUT scale can be probed by gravitational waves are discussed.\",\n      \"arxiv_id\": \"2009.07294\",\n      \"arxiv_category\": \"hep-ph\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1103/RevModPhys.93.035004\",\n      \"publication_info\": \"Rev.Mod.Phys. 93 (2021) 3\",\n      \"journal\": \"Rev.Mod.Phys.\",\n      \"volume\": \"93\",\n      \"year\": 2021,\n      \"pages\": \"3\",\n      \"citation_count\": 264,\n      \"citation_count_without_self\": 261,\n      \"date\": \"2020-09-15\",\n      \"inspire_url\": \"https://inspirehep.net/literature/1817513\",\n      \"arxiv_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.07294\",\n      \"keywords\": [\n        \"scale: grand unified theory\",\n        \"baryon: asymmetry\",\n        \"CP: violation\",\n        \"scale: electroweak interaction\",\n        \"neutrino: right-handed\",\n        \"neutrino: mass\",\n        \"grand unified theory: SU(5)\",\n        \"leptogenesis: thermal\",\n        \"electroweak interaction: symmetry breaking\",\n        \"lepton: asymmetry\",\n        \"Higgs model: composite\",\n        \"temperature: high\",\n        \"leptoquark: decay\",\n        \"plasma: thermal\",\n        \"baryogenesis\",\n        \"sphaleron\",\n        \"gravitational radiation\",\n        \"critical phenomena\",\n        \"interpretation of experiments: CERN LHC Coll\",\n        \"seesaw model\",\n        \"oscillation\"\n      ],\n      \"document_type\": \"article\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"recid\": \"1854533\",\n      \"title\": \"Reading the footprints of the B-meson flavor anomalies\",\n      \"authors\": [\n        \"Cornella, Claudia\",\n        \"Faroughy, Darius A.\",\n        \"Fuentes-Martin, Javier\",\n        \"Isidori, Gino\",\n        \"Neubert, Matthias\"\n      ],\n      \"abstract\": \"Motivated by the recent LHCb announcement of a 3.1\\u03c3 violation of lepton- flavor universality in the ratio R$_{K}$ = \\u0393(B \\u2192 K\\u03bc$^{+}$\\u03bc$^{\\u2212}$)/\\u0393(B \\u2192 Ke$^{+}$e$^{\\u2212}$), we present an updated, comprehensive analysis of the flavor anomalies seen in both neutral-current (b \\u2192 s\\u2113$^{+}$\\u2113$^{\\u2212}$) and charged-current (b \\u2192 $ c\\\\tau \\\\overline{\\\\nu} $) decays of B mesons. Our study starts from a model-independent effective field-theory approach and then considers both a simplified model and a UV-complete extension of the Standard Model featuring a vector leptoquark U$_{1}$ as the main mediator of the anomalies. We show that the new LHCb data corroborate the emerging pattern of a new, predominantly left-handed, semileptonic current-current interaction with a flavor structure respecting a (minimally) broken U(2)$^{5}$ flavor symmetry. New aspects of our analysis include a combined analysis of the semileptonic operators involving tau leptons, including in particular the important constraint from B$_{s}$-$ {\\\\overline{B}}_s $ mixing, a systematic study of the effects of right-handed leptoquark couplings and of deviations from minimal flavor-symmetry breaking, a detailed analysis of various rare B-decay modes which would provide smoking-gun signatures of this non-standard framework (LFV decays, di-tau modes, and B \\u2192 K$^{(*)} \\\\nu \\\\overline{\\\\nu} $), and finally an updated analysis of collider bounds on the leptoquark mass and couplings.\",\n      \"arxiv_id\": \"2103.16558\",\n      \"arxiv_category\": \"hep-ph\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1007/JHEP08(2021)050\",\n      \"publication_info\": \"JHEP 08 (2021) 050\",\n      \"journal\": \"JHEP\",\n      \"volume\": \"08\",\n      \"year\": 2021,\n      \"pages\": \"050\",\n      \"citation_count\": 227,\n      \"citation_count_without_self\": 198,\n      \"date\": \"2021-03-30\",\n      \"inspire_url\": \"https://inspirehep.net/literature/1854533\",\n      \"arxiv_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.16558\",\n      \"keywords\": [\n        \"Beyond Standard Model\",\n        \"Heavy Quark Physics\",\n        \"leptoquark: coupling\",\n        \"mediation: anomaly\",\n        \"symmetry: flavor\",\n        \"leptoquark: mass\",\n        \"LHC-B\",\n        \"effective field theory\",\n        \"charged current\",\n        \"neutral current\",\n        \"universality\",\n        \"right-handed\",\n        \"left-handed\",\n        \"signature\",\n        \"structure\",\n        \"mixing\",\n        \"meson\",\n        \"symmetry: U(2)\"\n      ],\n      \"document_type\": \"article\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"recid\": \"1618012\",\n      \"title\": \"Baryon Number and Lepton Universality Violation in Leptoquark and Diquark Models\",\n      \"authors\": [\n        \"Assad, Nima\",\n        \"Fornal, Bartosz\",\n        \"Grinstein, Benjamin\"\n      ],\n      \"abstract\": \"We perform a systematic study of models involving leptoquarks and diquarks with masses well below the grand unification scale and demonstrate that a large class of them is excluded due to rapid proton decay. After singling out the few phenomenologically viable color triplet and sextet scenarios, we show that there exist only two leptoquark models which do not suffer from tree-level proton decay and which have the potential for explaining the recently discovered anomalies in B meson decays. Both of those models, however, contain dimension five operators contributing to proton decay and require a new symmetry forbidding them to emerge at a higher scale. This has a particularly nice realization for the model with the vector leptoquark (3,1)2/3 , which points to a specific extension of the Standard Model, namely the Pati\\u2013Salam unification model, where this leptoquark naturally arises as the new gauge boson. We explore this possibility in light of recent B physics measurements. Finally, we analyze also a vector diquark model, discussing its LHC phenomenology and showing that it has nontrivial predictions for neutron\\u2013antineutron oscillation experiments.\",\n      \"arxiv_id\": \"1708.06350\",\n      \"arxiv_category\": \"hep-ph\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1016/j.physletb.2017.12.042\",\n      \"publication_info\": \"Phys.Lett.B 777 (2018) 324\",\n      \"journal\": \"Phys.Lett.B\",\n      \"volume\": \"777\",\n      \"year\": 2018,\n      \"pages\": \"324\",\n      \"citation_count\": 223,\n      \"citation_count_without_self\": 214,\n      \"date\": \"2017-08-21\",\n      \"inspire_url\": \"https://inspirehep.net/literature/1618012\",\n      \"arxiv_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.06350\",\n      \"keywords\": [\n        \"Proton decay\",\n        \"Leptoquarks\",\n        \"Pati\\u2013Salam unification\",\n        \"B anomalies\",\n        \"Diquarks\",\n        \"n\\u2212n\\u00af oscillations\",\n        \"[formula omitted] oscillations\",\n        \"p: decay\",\n        \"B: decay\",\n        \"diquark: vector\",\n        \"lepton: universality: violation\",\n        \"baryon number: violation\",\n        \"scale: grand unified theory\",\n        \"color: triplet\",\n        \"dimension: 5\",\n        \"leptoquark\",\n        \"quark model\",\n        \"CERN LHC Coll\",\n        \"gauge boson: new particle\",\n        \"anomaly\",\n        \"color: sextet\",\n        \"new physics\"\n      ],\n      \"document_type\": \"article\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"recid\": \"1681015\",\n      \"title\": \"Importance of Loop Effects in Explaining the Accumulated Evidence for New Physics in B Decays with a Vector Leptoquark\",\n      \"authors\": [\n        \"Crivellin, Andreas\",\n        \"Greub, Christoph\",\n        \"M\\u00fcller, Dario\",\n        \"Saturnino, Francesco\"\n      ],\n      \"abstract\": \"In recent years experiments revealed intriguing hints for new physics (NP) in B decays involving b\\u2192c\\u03c4\\u03bd and b\\u2192s\\u2113+\\u2113- transitions at the 4\\u03c3 and 5\\u03c3 level, respectively. In addition, there are slight disagreements in b\\u2192u\\u03c4\\u03bd and b\\u2192d\\u03bc+\\u03bc- observables. While not significant on their own, they point in the same direction. Furthermore, Vus extracted from \\u03c4 decays shows a slight tension (\\u22482.5\\u03c3) with its value determined from Cabibbo-Kobayashi-Maskawa unitarity, and an analysis of BELLE data found an excess in Bd\\u2192\\u03c4+\\u03c4-. Concerning NP explanations, the vector leptoquark SU(2) singlet is of special interest since it is the only single particle extension of the standard model which can (in principle) address all the anomalies described above. For this purpose, large couplings to \\u03c4 leptons are necessary and loop effects, which we calculate herein, become important. Including them in our phenomenological analysis, we find that neither the tension in Vus nor the excess in Bd\\u2192\\u03c4+\\u03c4- can be fully explained without violating bounds from K\\u2192\\u03c0\\u03bd\\u03bd\\u00af. However, one can account for b\\u2192c\\u03c4\\u03bd and b\\u2192u\\u03c4\\u03bd data finding intriguing correlations with Bq\\u2192\\u03c4+\\u03c4- and K\\u2192\\u03c0\\u03bd\\u03bd\\u00af. Furthermore, the explanation of b\\u2192c\\u03c4\\u03bd predicts a positive shift in C7 and a negative one in C9, being nicely in agreement with the global fit to b\\u2192s\\u2113+\\u2113- data. Finally, we point out that one can fully account for b\\u2192c\\u03c4\\u03bd and b\\u2192s\\u2113+\\u2113- without violating bounds from \\u03c4\\u2192\\u03d5\\u03bc, \\u03d2\\u2192\\u03c4\\u03bc, or b\\u2192s\\u03c4\\u03bc processes.\",\n      \"arxiv_id\": \"1807.02068\",\n      \"arxiv_category\": \"hep-ph\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1103/PhysRevLett.122.011805\",\n      \"publication_info\": \"Phys.Rev.Lett. 122 (2019)\",\n      \"journal\": \"Phys.Rev.Lett.\",\n      \"volume\": \"122\",\n      \"year\": 2019,\n      \"citation_count\": 207,\n      \"citation_count_without_self\": 172,\n      \"date\": \"2018-07-05\",\n      \"inspire_url\": \"https://inspirehep.net/literature/1681015\",\n      \"arxiv_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.02068\",\n      \"keywords\": [\n        \"13.20.He\",\n        \"13.25.Es\",\n        \"13.35.Dx\",\n        \"14.80.Sv\",\n        \"Elementary Particles and Fields\",\n        \"B: semileptonic decay\",\n        \"B: branching ratio\",\n        \"branching ratio: ratio\",\n        \"effective Lagrangian\",\n        \"higher-order\",\n        \"leptoquark: vector particle\",\n        \"unitarity\",\n        \"penguin\",\n        \"CKM matrix\",\n        \"BELLE\",\n        \"numerical calculations\",\n        \"new physics\"\n      ],\n      \"document_type\": \"article\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"recid\": \"481286\",\n      \"title\": \"HERA collider physics\",\n      \"authors\": [\n        \"Abramowicz, H.\",\n        \"Caldwell, A.\"\n      ],\n      \"abstract\": \"HERA, the first electron-proton collider, has been delivering luminosity since 1992. It is the natural extension of an impressive series of fixed-target lepton-nucleon scattering experiments. The increase of a factor ten in center-of-mass energy over that available for fixed-target experiments has allowed the discovery of several important results, such as the large number of slow partons in the proton, and the sizeable diffractive cross section at large $Q^2$. Recent data point to a possible deviation from Standard Model expectations at very high $Q^2$, highlighting the physics potential of HERA for new effects. The HERA program is currently in a transition period. The first six years of data taking have primarily elucidated the structure of the proton, allowed detailed QCD studies and had a strong impact on the understanding of QCD dynamics. The coming years will bring the era of electroweak studies and high $Q^2$ measurements. This is therefore an appropriate juncture at which to review HERA results.\",\n      \"arxiv_id\": \"hep-ex/9903037\",\n      \"arxiv_category\": \"hep-ex\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1103/RevModPhys.71.1275\",\n      \"publication_info\": \"Rev.Mod.Phys. 71 (1999) 1275\",\n      \"journal\": \"Rev.Mod.Phys.\",\n      \"volume\": \"71\",\n      \"year\": 1999,\n      \"pages\": \"1275\",\n      \"citation_count\": 206,\n      \"citation_count_without_self\": 197,\n      \"date\": \"1998-12\",\n      \"inspire_url\": \"https://inspirehep.net/literature/481286\",\n      \"arxiv_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ex/9903037\",\n      \"keywords\": [\n        \"review\",\n        \"electron p: colliding beams\",\n        \"positron p: colliding beams\",\n        \"electron p: interaction\",\n        \"exchange: one-photon\",\n        \"photon p: interaction\",\n        \"hadron: electroproduction\",\n        \"heavy quark\",\n        \"vector meson: photoproduction\",\n        \"jet\",\n        \"cross section\",\n        \"p: structure function\",\n        \"small-x\",\n        \"gluon: momentum spectrum\",\n        \"diffraction\",\n        \"quark model parton\",\n        \"Regge poles\",\n        \"pomeron\",\n        \"new particle: search for\",\n        \"new interaction: search for\",\n        \"leptoquark\",\n        \"leptogluon\",\n        \"sparticle\",\n        \"supersymmetry\",\n        \"fermion: excited state\",\n        \"quantum chromodynamics: perturbation theory\",\n        \"H1\",\n        \"ZEUS\",\n        \"experimental results\",\n        \"DESY HERA Stor\",\n        \"bibliography\",\n        \"approx. 300 GeV-cms\"\n      ],\n      \"document_type\": \"article\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"recid\": \"1797226\",\n      \"title\": \"Vector boson fusion at multi-TeV muon colliders\",\n      \"authors\": [\n        \"Costantini, Antonio\",\n        \"De Lillo, Federico\",\n        \"Maltoni, Fabio\",\n        \"Mantani, Luca\",\n        \"Mattelaer, Olivier\",\n        \"Ruiz, Richard\",\n        \"Zhao, Xiaoran\"\n      ],\n      \"abstract\": \"High-energy lepton colliders with a centre-of-mass energy in the multi-TeV range are currently considered among the most challenging and far-reaching future accelerator projects. Studies performed so far have mostly focused on the reach for new phenomena in lepton-antilepton annihilation channels. In this work we observe that starting from collider energies of a few TeV, electroweak (EW) vector boson fusion/scattering (VBF) at lepton colliders becomes the dominant production mode for all Standard Model processes relevant to studying the EW sector. In many cases we find that this also holds for new physics. We quantify the size and the growth of VBF cross sections with collider energy for a number of SM and new physics processes. By considering luminosity scenarios achievable at a muon collider, we conclude that such a machine would effectively be a \\u201chigh-luminosity weak boson collider,\\u201d and subsequently offer a wide range of opportunities to precisely measure EW and Higgs couplings as well as discover new particles.\",\n      \"arxiv_id\": \"2005.10289\",\n      \"arxiv_category\": \"hep-ph\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1007/JHEP09(2020)080\",\n      \"publication_info\": \"JHEP 09 (2020) 080\",\n      \"journal\": \"JHEP\",\n      \"volume\": \"09\",\n      \"year\": 2020,\n      \"pages\": \"080\",\n      \"citation_count\": 199,\n      \"citation_count_without_self\": 176,\n      \"date\": \"2020-05-20\",\n      \"inspire_url\": \"https://inspirehep.net/literature/1797226\",\n      \"arxiv_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.10289\",\n      \"keywords\": [\n        \"Beyond Standard Model\",\n        \"Effective Field Theories\",\n        \"Higgs Physics\",\n        \"vector boson: fusion\",\n        \"muon: storage ring\",\n        \"lepton antilepton: annihilation\",\n        \"Higgs particle: coupling\",\n        \"new physics\",\n        \"lepton lepton: colliding beams\",\n        \"energy: high\",\n        \"electroweak interaction\",\n        \"new particle\",\n        \"standard model\",\n        \"vector boson: scattering\",\n        \"scale: TeV\"\n      ],\n      \"document_type\": \"conference paper\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"recid\": \"2051251\",\n      \"title\": \"The physics case of a 3 TeV muon collider stage\",\n      \"authors\": [\n        \"de Blas, Jorge\",\n        \"Buttazzo, Dario\",\n        \"Capdevilla Roldan, Rodolfo Mario\",\n        \"Curtin, David\",\n        \"Franceschini, Roberto\",\n        \"Maltoni, Fabio\",\n        \"Meade, Patrick Roddy\",\n        \"Meloni, Federico\",\n        \"Su, Shufang\",\n        \"Vryonidou, Eleni\",\n        \"Wulzer, Andrea\",\n        \"Aime, Chiara\",\n        \"Apyan, Aram\",\n        \"Asadi, Pouya\",\n        \"Mahmoud Mohammed, Mohammed Attia\",\n        \"Azatov, Aleksandr T\",\n        \"Bartosik, Nazar\",\n        \"Bertolin, Alessandro\",\n        \"Bottaro, Salvatore\",\n        \"Buonincontri, Laura\",\n        \"Casarsa, Massimo\",\n        \"Castelli, Luca\",\n        \"Catanesi, Maria Gabriella\",\n        \"Celiberto, Francesco Giovanni\",\n        \"Cerri, Alessandro\",\n        \"Cesarotti, Cari\",\n        \"Chachamis, Grigorios\",\n        \"Chen, Siyu\",\n        \"Chien, Yang-Ting\",\n        \"Chiesa, Mauro\",\n        \"Costa, Marco\",\n        \"Da Molin, Giacomo\",\n        \"Dasu, Sridhara Rao\",\n        \"Denisov, Dmitri S\",\n        \"Denizli, Haluk\",\n        \"Dermisek, Radovan\",\n        \"Di Luzio, Luca\",\n        \"Di Micco, Biagio\",\n        \"Dienes, Keith R\",\n        \"Dorigo, Tommaso\",\n        \"Fabbrichesi, Marco Ernesto Renato\",\n        \"Fiorina, Davide\",\n        \"Forslund, Matthew\",\n        \"Gabrielli, Emidio\",\n        \"Garosi, Francesco\",\n        \"Glioti, Alfredo\",\n        \"Greco, Mario\",\n        \"Greljo, Admir\",\n        \"Grober, Ramona\",\n        \"Grojean, Christophe\",\n        \"Gu, Jiayin\",\n        \"Han, Chengcheng\",\n        \"Han, Cheng Cheng\",\n        \"Han, Tao\",\n        \"Hermanek, Keith\",\n        \"Herndon, Matthew Fairbanks\",\n        \"Holmes, Tova Ray\",\n        \"Homiller, Samuel\",\n        \"Huang, Guoyuan\",\n        \"Huang, Guo-yuan\",\n        \"Jana, Sudip\",\n        \"Jindariani, Sergo Robert\",\n        \"Kahn, Yonatan\",\n        \"Kilian, Wolfgang\",\n        \"Koppenburg, Patrick Stefan\",\n        \"Kreher, Nils\",\n        \"Krizka, Karol\",\n        \"Krnjaic, Gordan Zdenko\",\n        \"Kumar, Nilanjana\",\n        \"Lee, Lawrence\",\n        \"Li, Qiang\",\n        \"Liu, Zhen\",\n        \"Long, Kenneth Richard\",\n        \"Low, Ian\",\n        \"Lu, Qianshu\",\n        \"Lucchesi, Donatella\",\n        \"Ma, Lian Liang\",\n        \"Ma, Yang\",\n        \"Mantani, Luca\",\n        \"Marzocca, David\",\n        \"McGinnis, Navin\",\n        \"Mele, Barbara\",\n        \"Merlassino, Claudia\",\n        \"Montella, Alessandro\",\n        \"Nardecchia, M\",\n        \"Nardi, Federico\",\n        \"Panci, Paolo\",\n        \"Pagan Griso, Simone\",\n        \"Panico, Giuliano\",\n        \"Paradisi, Paride\",\n        \"Pastrone, Nadia\",\n        \"Piccinini, Fulvio\",\n        \"Potamianos, Karolos Jozef\",\n        \"Radicioni, Emilio\",\n        \"Rattazzi, Riccardo\",\n        \"Redigolo, Diego\",\n        \"Reina, Laura\",\n        \"Reuter, J\\u00fcrgen\",\n        \"Riccardi, Cristina\",\n        \"Ricci, Lorenzo\",\n        \"Ristori, Luciano\",\n        \"Robens, Tania Natalie\",\n        \"Rodejohann, Werner\",\n        \"Ruiz, Richard Efrain\",\n        \"Queiroz, Farinaldo S\",\n        \"Sala, Filippo\",\n        \"\\u0160alko, Jakub\",\n        \"Salvini, Paola\",\n        \"Santiago Perez, Jose\",\n        \"Tait, Tim M.P.\",\n        \"Sarra, Ivano\",\n        \"Schulte, Daniel\",\n        \"Selvaggi, Michele\",\n        \"Senol, Abdulkadir\",\n        \"Sestini, Lorenzo\",\n        \"Sharma, Varun\",\n        \"Simoniello, Rosa\",\n        \"Stark, Giordon Holtsberg\",\n        \"Stolarski, Daniel\",\n        \"Su, Wei\",\n        \"Sumensari, Olcyr\",\n        \"Sun, Xiaohu\",\n        \"Tait, Timothy Maurice Paul\",\n        \"Tang, Jian\",\n        \"Tesi, Andrea\",\n        \"Thomas, Brooks\",\n        \"Thompson, Emily Anne\",\n        \"Torre, Riccardo\",\n        \"Trifinopoulos, Sokratis\",\n        \"Vai, Ilaria\",\n        \"Valenti, Alessandro\",\n        \"Vittorio, Ludovico\",\n        \"Wang, Lian-Tao\",\n        \"Wu, Yongcheng\",\n        \"Xie, Keping\",\n        \"Zhao, Xiaoran\",\n        \"Zurita, Jos\\u00e9 Francisco\"\n      ],\n      \"abstract\": \"In the path towards a muon collider with center of mass energy of 10 TeV or more, a stage at 3 TeV emerges as an appealing option. Reviewing the physics potential of such muon collider is the main purpose of this document. In order to outline the progression of the physics performances across the stages, a few sensitivity projections for higher energy are also presented. There are many opportunities for probing new physics at a 3 TeV muon collider. Some of them are in common with the extensively documented physics case of the CLIC 3 TeV energy stage, and include measuring the Higgs trilinear coupling and testing the possible composite nature of the Higgs boson and of the top quark at the 20 TeV scale. Other opportunities are unique of a 3 TeV muon collider, and stem from the fact that muons are collided rather than electrons. This is exemplified by studying the potential to explore the microscopic origin of the current $g$-2 and $B$-physics anomalies, which are both related with muons.\",\n      \"arxiv_id\": \"2203.07261\",\n      \"arxiv_category\": \"hep-ph\",\n      \"citation_count\": 168,\n      \"citation_count_without_self\": 79,\n      \"date\": \"2022-03-14\",\n      \"inspire_url\": \"https://inspirehep.net/literature/2051251\",\n      \"arxiv_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.07261\",\n      \"keywords\": [\n        \"Higgs particle: coupling\",\n        \"effective field theory\",\n        \"Higgs particle: doublet: 2\",\n        \"minimal supersymmetric standard model\",\n        \"muon: magnetic moment\",\n        \"leptoquark\",\n        \"lepton: flavor: violation\",\n        \"coupling: Yukawa\",\n        \"fermion: vector particle\",\n        \"sensitivity\",\n        \"muon: storage ring\",\n        \"energy: high\",\n        \"CERN CLIC\",\n        \"numerical calculations\",\n        \"new physics\"\n      ],\n      \"collaborations\": [\n        \"Muon Collider\"\n      ],\n      \"document_type\": \"article\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"recid\": \"1701635\",\n      \"title\": \"Search for heavy neutrinos and third-generation leptoquarks in hadronic states of two $\\\\tau$ leptons and two jets in proton-proton collisions at $\\\\sqrt{s} =$ 13 TeV\",\n      \"authors\": [\n        \"Sirunyan, Albert M\",\n        \"Tumasyan, Armen\",\n        \"Adam, Wolfgang\",\n        \"Ambrogi, Federico\",\n        \"Asilar, Ece\",\n        \"Bergauer, Thomas\",\n        \"Brandstetter, Johannes\",\n        \"Dragicevic, Marko\",\n        \"Er\\u00f6, Janos\",\n        \"Escalante Del Valle, Alberto\",\n        \"Flechl, Martin\",\n        \"Fruehwirth, Rudolf\",\n        \"Ghete, Vasile Mihai\",\n        \"Hrubec, Josef\",\n        \"Jeitler,