{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"heptapod","tool":"Inspiresearch","arguments":{"query":"Physics of leptoquarks in precision experiments and at particle colliders","sort":"mostcited","size":5},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"schema\": \"inspire-hep-1.0\",\n  \"total\": 3,\n  \"returned\": 3,\n  \"papers\": [\n    {\n      \"recid\": \"1428667\",\n      \"title\": \"Physics of leptoquarks in precision experiments and at particle colliders\",\n      \"authors\": [\n        \"Dor\\u0161ner, I.\",\n        \"Fajfer, S.\",\n        \"Greljo, A.\",\n        \"Kamenik, J.F.\",\n        \"Ko\\u0161nik, N.\"\n      ],\n      \"abstract\": \"We present a comprehensive review of physics effects generated by leptoquarks (LQs), i.e.,\\u00a0hypothetical particles that can turn quarks into leptons and vice versa, of either scalar or vector nature. These considerations include discussion of possible completions of the Standard Model that contain LQ fields. The main focus of the review is on those LQ scenarios that are not problematic with regard to proton stability. We accordingly concentrate on the phenomenology of light leptoquarks that is relevant for precision experiments and particle colliders. Important constraints on LQ interactions with matter are derived from precision low-energy observables such as electric dipole moments, ( g\\u22122 ) of charged leptons, atomic parity violation, neutral meson mixing, Kaon, B , and D meson decays, etc. We provide a general analysis of indirect constraints on the strength of LQ interactions with the quarks and leptons to make statements that are as model independent as possible. We address complementary constraints that originate from electroweak precision measurements, top, and Higgs physics. The Higgs physics analysis we present covers not only the most recent but also expected results from the Large Hadron Collider (LHC). We finally discuss direct LQ searches. Current experimental situation is summarized and self-consistency of assumptions that go into existing accelerator-based searches is discussed. A progress in making next-to-leading order predictions for both pair and single LQ productions at colliders is also outlined.\",\n      \"arxiv_id\": \"1603.04993\",\n      \"arxiv_category\": \"hep-ph\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1016/j.physrep.2016.06.001\",\n      \"publication_info\": \"Phys.Rept. 641 (2016) 1\",\n      \"journal\": \"Phys.Rept.\",\n      \"volume\": \"641\",\n      \"year\": 2016,\n      \"pages\": \"1\",\n      \"citation_count\": 766,\n      \"citation_count_without_self\": 701,\n      \"date\": \"2016-03-16\",\n      \"inspire_url\": \"https://inspirehep.net/literature/1428667\",\n      \"arxiv_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.04993\",\n      \"keywords\": [\n        \"interaction: Yukawa\",\n        \"parity: violation\",\n        \"dipole: electric\",\n        \"meson: mixing\",\n        \"meson: decay\",\n        \"energy: low\",\n        \"higher-order: 1\",\n        \"leptoquark\",\n        \"electroweak interaction\",\n        \"lepton: magnetic moment\",\n        \"CERN LHC Coll\"\n      ],\n      \"document_type\": \"article\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"recid\": \"885415\",\n      \"title\": \"Particle physics experiments at high energy colliders\",\n      \"authors\": [\n        \"Hauptman, John\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_count\": 3,\n      \"citation_count_without_self\": 3,\n      \"date\": \"2011\",\n      \"inspire_url\": \"https://inspirehep.net/literature/885415\",\n      \"keywords\": [\n        \"book\",\n        \"standard model\",\n        \"detector\",\n        \"particle identification\",\n        \"accelerator\",\n        \"sociology\",\n        \"bibliography\"\n      ],\n      \"document_type\": \"book\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"recid\": \"1423391\",\n      \"title\": \"Study of New Silicon Sensors for Experiments at Future Particle Colliders\",\n      \"authors\": [\n        \"Munoz Sanchez, Francisca Javiela\"\n      ],\n      \"abstract\": \"In this work, two new technologies for future tracker detectors at future colliders are studied. In addition, the characterization techniques are described and the obtained results are presented.On one side, we studied two-dimensional position-sensitive microstrip sensors. This sensors use a resistive material as electrode instead of the standard metallic one. In this way, using a single sensor we can get information about two coordinates of a particle hit.On the other side, we studied double-sided double-type 3D pixel sensors. This sensors are manufactured in 3D technology instead of in the planar technology. They show more radiation hardness and require less energy to be efficiently operated than sensors manufactured in planar technology.With this work, we demonstrate the resistive microstrip sensors functionality as particle detector and the radiation hardness of 3D pixel detectors has been evaluated.\",\n      \"citation_count\": 0,\n      \"citation_count_without_self\": 0,\n      \"date\": \"2014\",\n      \"inspire_url\": \"https://inspirehep.net/literature/1423391\",\n      \"keywords\": [\n        \"Detectores semiconductores\",\n        \"Detectores de radiaci\\u00f3n\",\n        \"Detectores de part\\u00edculas\",\n        \"thesis\",\n        \"semiconductor detector: pixel\",\n        \"measurement methods\",\n        \"laser\",\n        \"numerical calculations\",\n        \"noise\"\n      ],\n      \"document_type\": \"thesis\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"query_info\": {\n    \"original\": \"Physics of leptoquarks in precision experiments and at particle colliders\",\n    \"spires\": \"t experiments and at particle colliders\",\n    \"sort\": \"mostcited\",\n    \"size\": 5\n  }\n}"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
