{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '123,135p' examples/demo_codex_research/text/1603.04993_source.tex\"","aggregated_output":"We adopt a notation that was introduced in Ref.~\\cite{Buchmuller:1986zs} to denote individual leptoquark multiplets under the SM gauge group and provide a list of all possible LQs in Table~\\ref{tab:LQs}. There are, all in all, six scalar ($S_3$, $R_2$, $\\tilde{R}_2$, $\\tilde{S}_1$, $S_1$, and $\\bar{S}_1$) and six vector ($U_3$, $V_2$, $\\tilde{V}_2$, $\\tilde{U}_1$, $U_1$, and $\\bar{U}_1$) LQ multiplets if one uses transformations under the SM gauge group as the classification criterion. In the first column of Table~\\ref{tab:LQs} we explicitly specify the SM transformation properties that can be easily understood on purely group theoretical grounds as follows. \n\\begin{table}[tbp]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tabular}{|c|cccc|}\n\\hline\n$(SU(3),SU(2),U(1))$ &  Spin & Symbol & Type & $F$ \\\\\n\\hline \n$(\\overline{\\mathbf{3}},\\mathbf{3},1/3)$ & 0 & $S_3$ & $LL$\\,$(S^L_1)$ & $-2$ \\\\\n$(\\mathbf{3},\\mathbf{2},7/6)$ & 0 & $R_2$ & $RL$\\,$(S^L_{1/2})$, $LR$\\,$(S^R_{1/2})$ & $0$ \\\\\n$(\\mathbf{3},\\mathbf{2},1/6)$ & 0 & $\\tilde{R}_2$ & $RL$\\,$(\\tilde{S}^L_{1/2})$, $\\overline{LR}$\\,$(\\tilde{S}^{\\overline{L}}_{1/2})$ & $0$ \\\\\n$(\\overline{\\mathbf{3}},\\mathbf{1},4/3)$ & 0 & $\\tilde{S}_1$ & $RR$\\,$(\\tilde{S}^R_{0})$ & $-2$ \\\\\n$(\\overline{\\mathbf{3}},\\mathbf{1},1/3)$ & 0 & $S_1$ & $LL$\\,$(S^L_0)$, $RR$\\,$(S^R_0)$, $\\overline{RR}$\\,$(S^{\\overline{R}}_0)$ & $-2$ \\\\\n$(\\overline{\\mathbf{3}},\\mathbf{1},-2/3)$ & 0 & $\\bar{S}_1$ & $\\overline{RR}$\\,$(\\bar{S}^{\\overline{R}}_0)$ & $-2$ \\\\\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_39","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '258,266p' examples/demo_codex_research/text/1603.04993_source.tex\"","aggregated_output":"\\subsection{$S_1=(\\overline{\\mathbf{3}},\\mathbf{1},1/3)$}\n\nThere are five possible operators that describe how $S_1$ interacts with fermions. These are\n\\begin{align}\n\\nonumber\n\\mathcal{L} \\supset &+y^{LL}_{1\\,ij}\\bar{Q}_{L}^{C\\,i,a} S_{1} \\epsilon^{ab}L_{L}^{j,b}+y^{RR}_{1\\,ij}\\bar{u}_{R}^{C\\,i} S_{1} e_{R}^{j}+y^{\\overline{RR}}_{1\\,ij}\\bar{d}_{R}^{C\\,i} S_{1} \\nu_{R}^{j}+\\\\\n\\label{eq:main_S_1}\n&+z^{LL}_{1\\,ij}\\bar{Q}_{L}^{C\\,i,a} S^*_{1} \\epsilon^{ab}Q_{L}^{j,b}+z^{RR}_{1\\,ij}\\bar{u}_{R}^{C\\,i} S^*_{1} d_{R}^{j}+\\textrm{h.c.},\n\\end{align}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
